"Colonial administration records (migrated archives) 1st tranche (https://cdn.nationalarchives.gov.uk/documents/migrated-archives-guidance.pdf)\nciteturn719view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 77; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn717view0\",\"lineno\":1555}); Total lines: 3397\nL1434@P33: Related material in CO 67/253/10, 253/11\nL1435@P33: FCO 141/2603: libel action against the Governor by Ioannis Kyriakides, 1938.\nL1436@P33: Related material in CO 67/290/17, 303/3\nL1437@P33: FCO 141/2340, 2380, 2409, 2446, 2474, 2514, 2529, 2545, 2575, 2576, 2599,\nL1438@P33: 2604: legislation, orders in council 1907-37\nL1439@P33: Politics\nL1440@P33: FCO 141/2445, 2459, 2464-2647: reports on political developments 1926-32.\nL1441@P33: FCO 141/2401, 2414, 2430, 2629: memorials and petitions on union with Greece,\nL1442@P33: and on political liberties 1925-39.\nL1443@P33: FCO 141/2404, 2455, 2606: communism, 1926-38.\nL1444@P33: FCO 141/2343: subscriptions for Greek national purposes collected in Cyprus, 1910.\nL1445@P33: FCO 141/2360, 2385, 2438: union with Greece, 1914-30.\nL1446@P33: FCO 141/2384, 2386: peace celebrations and return of King Constantine to Greece,\nL1447@P33: 1920.\nL1448@P33: FCO 141/2394, 2457: anniversary of Greek Independence Day, 1922-32.\nL1449@P33: FCO 141/2395, 2400: Pan-Cypriot Assembly, 1921-23.\nL1450@P33: FCO 141/2398: political agitation by Muslims in Cyprus, 1922.\nL1451@P33: FCO 141/2410: documents and correspondence from Turkish Times in house of\nL1452@P33: Chief Cadi, 1927.\nL1453@P33: FCO 141/2532: formation of trades unions, 1936.\nL1454@P33: FCO 141/2543: policy towards Kemalism including the question of the wearing of\nL1455@P33: the fez, 1936.\nL1456@P33: FCO 141/2555: visit of Prince Andrew of Greece to Cyprus, April 1937.\nL1457@P33-34: FCO 141/2595: restriction on activities of Evcaf, 1938.Colonial administration records (migrated archives) guidance\nL1458@P34: Religious Affairs\nL1459@P34: FCO 141/2344, 2372, 2436, 2461, 2482, 2483, 2527: Archbishop of Cyprus (and\nL1460@P34: locum tenens) 1910-35.\nL1461@P34: FCO 141/2345, 2376, 2377, 2378, 2381, 2452, 2453: Bishops of Kitium, Kyrenia\nL1462@P34: and Paphos 1910-31.\nL1463@P34: FCO 141/2458, 2522: Ecumenical Patriarch of Constantinople 1932-35.\nL1464@P34: FCO 141/2511-2513, 2602: Assyrian Church 1934-38.\nL1465@P34: FCO 141/2428, 2557: Islam on Cyprus 1928-37.\nL1466@P34: FCO 141/2347: suggestions by members of Legislative Council for alleviating\nL1467@P34: supposed injustices to the Christian population, 1911.\nL1468@P34: FCO 141/2374: proposed substitution of the term ‘Orthodox’ for ‘Greek\nL1469@P34: Christian’, 1917.\nL1470@P34: FCO 141/2417: charitable activities of the Greek Church in Cyprus among the\nL1471@P34: poor; repairs to Leper Farm church, 1928.\nL1472@P34: FCO 141/2521: memorandum on administration of church property and\nL1473@P34: finances, 1934.\nL1474@P34: FCO 141/2554: report on the Christian Cypriot Brotherhood of St Barnabas,\nL1475@P34: Famagusta, 1936.\nL1476@P34: Government\nL1477@P34: FCO 141/2591: Cyprus Committee (London), 1938.\nL1478@P34: FCO 141/2349 - 2351, 2393, 2416, 2423, 2441, 2444: Greek and Turkish\nL1479@P34: members of Legislative\nL1480@P34: and Executive Councils,\nL1481@P34: 1912-31.\nL1482@P34: FCO 141/2361: vote against government by an official member of the\nL1483@P34: Legislative Council, 1915.\nL1484@P34: FCO 141/2478-81: Advisory Council minutes, 1933.\nL1485@P34: FCO 141/2421-22: proposals for constitutional reform, 1929.\nL1486@P34: FCO 141/2630: memorandum by the Chief Justice on the functions of the\nL1487@P34: Judiciary and the Executive, 1939.\nL1488@P34: FCO 141/2362, 2510, 2585, 2585: district administration, reports and\nL1489@P34: conferences 1915-39.\nL1490@P34: FCO 141/2403, 2429, 2454: government posts – creation, administration in\nL1491@P34: absence of governor, appointment of Cypriots to\nL1492@P34: senior posts 1926-31.\nL1493@P34: FCO 141/2525: administrative memoranda, 1936.\nL1494@P34: FCO 141/2633: reports by Public Information Officer, 1939.\nL1495@P34: External affairs and defence\nL1496@P34: FCO 141/2363: arrangements in regard to Evkaf Department on annexation of\nL1497@P34: Cyprus, 1915.\nL1498@P34: FCO 141/2375, 2473: foreign consuls in Cyprus 1917-32.\nL1499@P34: FCO 141/2348, 2387: recruitment into Turkish and Greek armies respectively,\nL1500@P34-35: 1911-20.Colonial administration records (migrated archives) guidance\nL1501@P35: FCO 141/2418, 2419, 2420: Defence Scheme, 1928-30.\nL1502@P35: FCO 141/2431: expenditure on Cyprus Military Police, 1929.\nL1503@P35: FCO 141/2476: stationing of troops in Cyprus, Imperial defence, grants-in-aid\nL1504@P35: and Turkish debt charge, 1933.\nL1505@P35: FCO 141/2546, 2631, 2362, 2634: recruitment of Cypriots into British forces\nL1506@P35: and formation of units, 1936-39.\nL1507@P35: FCO 141/2592, 2593, 2637: Royal Navy bombardment ranges 1938-39.\nL1508@P35: FCO 141/2594: emergency measures, 1938.\nL1509@P35: Finance, revenues and payments\nL1510@P35: FCO 141/2341, 2518: grants-in-aid from British Government, 1879-1910.\nL1511@P35: FCO 141/2346, 2358, 2405, 2442, 2443, 2601: estimates 1912-1939.\nL1512@P35: FCO 141/2406, 2411, 2434: taxation and tariffs 1926-29.\nL1513@P35: FCO 141/2407, 2541, 2583: banks and Cyprus government 1926-38.\nL1514@P35: FCO 141/2371: cheque Turkish Government through United States Minister at\nL1515@P35: Athens for payment to Cadi of Cyprus of his salary, 1916.\nL1516@P35: FCO 141/2408: claim by Ibn Saud to the revenues of Haramain Waqfs, 1927.\nL1517@P35: FCO 141/2528: application by British Government for refund of payments from\nL1518@P35: Cyprus revenue to Turkish debt, 1935.\nL1519@P35: Transport and Communications\nL1520@P35: FCO 141/2364, 2392, 2426, 2427, 2460, 2496, 2498, 2500, 2501, 2537-2539,\nL1521@P35: 2558, 2559, 2574, 2597, 2635: newspapers (articles, publishing, suppression,\nL1522@P35: censorship, government support) 1915-40\nL1523@P35: FCO 141/2424, 2425, 2432, 2515: aerodromes and harbours, 1928-29.\nL1524@P35: FCO 141/2577-80: establishment of a broadcasting station in Cyprus, 1937.\nL1525@P35: Commerce, trade, natural resources and antiquities\nL1526@P35: FCO 141/2439: ‘A brief study of the resources of Cyprus and the possibility of\nL1527@P35: their rapid development’, by R E Fisher, 1930.\nL1528@P35: FCO 141/2365, 2499, 2605, 2628: mines, mining companies and geological\nL1529@P35: surveys, 1915-39.\nL1530@P35: FCO 141/2412, 2596: forests and forestry, 1928-38.\nL1531@P35: FCO 141/2526: olive development, 1935.\nL1532@P35: FCO 141/2531, 2582, 2584, 2600: water supplies and irrigation, 1936-38.\nL1533@P35: FCO 141/2355, 2370, 2544, 2556: land purchase, registration and rents of\nL1534@P35: property 1912-37.\nL1535@P35: FCO 141/2573: employment of labour, 1937.\nL1536@P35: FCO 141/2598: import of gramophone records of Greek national anthem, 1938.\nL1537@P35-36: FCO 141/2638: marketing of Commanderia wine in the UK, 1939.Colonial administration records (migrated archives) guidance\nL1538@P36: FCO 141/2415, 2475, 2520, 2533, 2535: antiquities 1928-36.\nL1539@P36: Education\nL1540@P36: FCO 141/2356, 2519, 2536: reports on education in Cyprus, 1913-36 (2536 also\nL1541@P36: includes town planning)\nL1542@P36: FCO 141/2359: construction of schools 1913.\nL1543@P36: FCO 141/2382: British Boys Scouts Association, 1919.\nL1544@P36: FCO 141/2396, 2437, 2509: Muslim and Turkish education 1921-34.\nL1545@P36: FCO 141/2399: memorial protesting against the proposed amendment of the\nL1546@P36: Education Law for Greek Christian elementary schools, 1923.\nL1547@P36: FCO 141/2433: assumption of control of elementary school teachers by the\nL1548@P36: Education Department, 1929.\nL1549@P36: FCO 141/2468: English lessons for Greek and Turkish school teachers, 1932.\nL1550@P36: FCO 141/2469-72: secondary education in Cyprus, 1932.\nL1551@P36: FCO 141/2494-95: payments of pensions to schoolmasters in Cyprus by the\nL1552@P36: Greek Government, 1933.\nL1553@P36: Immigration\nL1554@P36: FCO 141/2389-91: immigration of Jews, Maltese and Assyrians into Cyprus,\nL1555@P36: 1920.\nL1556@P36: FCO 141/2523-24: Cypriot community in London, including question of\nL1557@P36: passport facilities, 1935.\nL1558@P36: FCO 141/2517: deportation of British subjects from the colonies; Law Number\nL1559@P36-37: 17 of 1936; report of departmental committee, 1934.Colonial administration records (migrated archives): Kenya\nL1560@P37: FCO 141/5502-5530, 5646A-5650, 5706-5717, 6086-6123, 6291-6294, 6349-\nL1561@P37: 6359, 6383-6392, 6405-6408, 6412-6413, 6620-6738, 6766-6782, 6918-6921,\nL1562@P37: 6949-6965, 7049-7061, 7071-7084, 7089-7136, 7164-7169\nL1563@P37: The1886 Anglo-German Agreement placed the\nL1564@P37: region largely within Kenya’s modern borders in an\nL1565@P37: area of British influence, and the Imperial British\nL1566@P37: East Africa Company was set up to exploit this. In\nL1567@P37: 1895 the East Africa Protectorate was established\nL1568@P37: taking over from the defunct IBEA Company\nL1569@P37: and in 1920 the Colony of Kenya was created,\nL1570@P37: with the coastal strip (originally a protectorate\nL1571@P37: of the Sultan of Zanzibar) remaining a British\nL1572@P37: protectorate. Following the Mau Mau uprising the\nL1573@P37: 1950s Kenya obtained independence in 1963.\nL1574@P37: The records are arranged below within subject\nL1575@P37: areas based, as much as is practical, on the\nL1576@P37: organisation of the colony’s government and on\nL1577@P37: its file registration systems. All subject areas have\nL1578@P37: been reviewed, providing additional description\nL1579@P37: and an indication of size and scope.\nL1580@P37: Note that it was administrative practice in Kenya\nL1581@P37: to attach correspondence to both inner sides of\nL1582@P37: the file docket. The left hand side usually contained\nL1583@P37: telegrams and other official communications and\nL1584@P37: the right hand side other correspondence, internal\nL1585@P37: reports and memoranda. Some files have been\nL1586@P37: housed in new dockets as part of the transfer\nL1587@P37: process and occasionally these two groups of\nL1588@P37: correspondence have been placed together on the\nL1589@P37: right hand side thus creating two chronological runs\nL1590@P37: one after another in the same file. All records are\nL1591@P37: treasury-tagged files except where stated.\nL1592@P37: Most of the records date from the period 1952-63,\nL1593@P37: with a small number (55 volumes or files) from\nL1594@P37: 1907-1952.\nL1595@P37: The guide is arranged under the following headings:\nL1596@P37: 1. Meeting minutes and memoranda: Colonial and\nL1597@P37: inter-colonial governance\nL1598@P37: 2. Governor’s Office\nL1599@P37: 3. Chief Secretary’s Office\nL1600@P37: 4. Secretariat\nL1601@P37: 5. Ministry of African Affairs\nL1602@P37: 6. Ministry of Defence\nL1603@P37-38: 7. Other recordsColonial administration records (migrated archives) guidance\nL1604@P38: 1. Meeting minutes and memoranda: colonial and inter-colonial\nL1605@P38: governance\nL1606@P38: Conferences of Governors of British East African Territories\nL1607@P38: FCO 141/5502-5514: bound hardback volumes (45cm thick in total, 1932-39)\nL1608@P38: of typed meeting minutes and memoranda. Each volume\nL1609@P38: is between 2 and 5cm thick.\nL1610@P38: Related material in CO 822/44/2, 44/3, 48/5, 48/6, 48/7, 60/8, 60/9, 60/10,\nL1611@P38: 65/3, 65/4, 65/5, 65/6, 65/7, 71/2, 78/2, 84/21, 93/11, 93/12.\nL1612@P38: FCO 141/5515-5517: bound hardback volumes (6cm thick in total, 1935-39)\nL1613@P38: similar to above, but relating to the Transport Policy\nL1614@P38: Boards at such conferences.\nL1615@P38: Related material in CO 822/67/11, 80/7, 86/13, 97/4\nL1616@P38: Proceedings of East Africa High Commission\nL1617@P38: FCO 141/6405 – 6408: (10cm thick in total, 1954-61) containing proceedings\nL1618@P38: of the East Africa High Commission; agenda, minutes and background papers\nL1619@P38: covering the 19 - 32 meetings.\nL1620@P38: Related material in CO 822/1202-1207 and CO 822/1866-1869.\nL1621@P38: Minutes of Executive and Legislative Councils of Colony of Kenya\nL1622@P38: FCO 141/5518-5530: bound hardback volumes (33cm thick in total, 1939-50)\nL1623@P38: of typed meeting minutes and memoranda. Each volume\nL1624@P38: is between 100 and 220 pages long. Volumes have subject\nL1625@P38: indexes at the front until 1946. FCO 141/5518 is a\nL1626@P38: duplicate of CO 544/58, the rest partially fills the gap in\nL1627@P38: ExCo minutes in CO 544\nL1628@P38: FCO 141/6704-6707, 6383-6392: files (c.13cm thick in total, 1955-61)\nL1629@P38: containing proceedings of the Kenya\nL1630@P38: Legislative Council. Issues include votes and\nL1631@P38: procedures, private members motions, the\nL1632@P38: composition of nominated members from\nL1633@P38: the Northern Frontier District, the election\nL1634@P38: of the speaker, forthcoming legislative\nL1635@P38: programme, written questions to the Council,\nL1636@P38: extension of voting rights and the future role\nL1637@P38: of European elected members.\nL1638@P38: Related material in CO 544.\nL1639@P38: 2. Governor’s Office\nL1640@P38: The records below are those of the Governor’s private office. The governors\nL1641@P38: of Kenya in the post-war period were: Sir Philip Mitchell (1944-52), Sir Evelyn\nL1642@P38: Baring (1952-57), Sir Frederick Crawford (1957-59), Sir Patrick Renison (1959-\nL1643@P38: 63) and Sir Malcolm MacDonald (1963).\nL1644@P38: Older records\nL1645@P38: FCO 141/5646A, 5646B, 5647, 5650: (1907-37) files on various subjects. Thin\nL1646@P38: files with fewer than 15 sheets of paper\nL1647@P38: each, relating to the local currency, railway\nL1648@P38: construction, oaths taken by governors\nL1649@P38: and native disturbances in Nyanza\nL1650@P38: respectively.\nL1651@P38: FCO 141/5648 and 5469: (2cm thick in total, 1930-37) files relating to the\nL1652@P38: possible purchase of the Kenya Protectorate from\nL1653@P38: Zanzibar, including the status of Zanzibar treaties\nL1654@P38-39: with France and the United States. Colonial administration records (migrated archives) guidance\nL1655@P39: FCO 141/6349: (1cm thick, 1941-43) relating to death of Duke of Aosta (former\nL1656@P39: Italian Viceroy in Abyssinia), mostly covers the drill and order\nL1657@P39: of service for his funeral and publicity/propaganda matters in\nL1658@P39: communicating his death.\nL1659@P39: Related material: CO 323/1857/18.\nL1660@P39: FCO 141/6350-6356: (15cm thick in total, 1927-44) relating to Ethiopia/Kenya\nL1661@P39: relations and border issues (FCO 141/6350 includes a\nL1662@P39: map of the border area). Subjects covered include internal\nL1663@P39: Ethiopian politics, subversive activities and border raids by\nL1664@P39: Ethiopians and British policy towards Ethiopia.\nL1665@P39: International organisations/conferences\nL1666@P39: FCO 141/7059, 7057\nL1667@P39: Two files (4cm thick in total, 1958-63) covering Kenyan matters at or relating\nL1668@P39: to the United Nations, including the visits of various UN committees and\nL1669@P39: officials to Kenya, correspondence and reports from UK representatives at the\nL1670@P39: UN concerning Kenya, the impact of publicity of certain resolutions (including\nL1671@P39: 1654 and 1810) and the UN Special Fund.\nL1672@P39: FCO 141/7059: includes a copy of the report of quadripartite talks\nL1673@P39: between Britain, France, Belgium and Portugal over Africa in\nL1674@P39: September 1959.\nL1675@P39: FCO 141/7057: includes a list of all UN officials resident in east and central\nL1676@P39: Africa.\nL1677@P39: Related material: CO 936/699.\nL1678@P39: FCO 141/7058, 7060: two files (c. 30 sheets of paper in total, 1963) covering a\nL1679@P39: regional cartographical conference and a meeting of the\nL1680@P39: International Olympic Committee respectively in 1963.\nL1681@P39: Both cover problems of South African (and in the former\nL1682@P39: case Portuguese as well) attendance at these events and\nL1683@P39: possible related disturbances.\nL1684@P39: Somalia and Northern Frontier District\nL1685@P39: FCO 141/7125-7136\nL1686@P39: Twelve files (33cm thick in total, 1960-63) relating to the legal position of the\nL1687@P39: Northern Frontier District, the decision to formally integrate it into Kenya on\nL1688@P39: independence and Somali claims to it.\nL1689@P39: FCO 141/7125-7130: relates to the future of the district and negotiations\nL1690@P39: with the Somalis, later files include regular situation\nL1691@P39: and incident reports on border raids and other\nL1692@P39: incidents in the district.\nL1693@P39: FCO 141/7131: covers British policy from April 1963 to independence and\nL1694@P39: raids by Somalis.\nL1695@P39: FCO 141/7132-7133: covers talks in Rome over the district, including notes\nL1696@P39: and telegrams back to Nairobi.\nL1697@P39: FCO 141/7134: covers British military and police operations in the area\nL1698@P39: prior to independence to forestall Somali raids or attempts\nL1699@P39: at annexation; includes details of RAF and police radio\nL1700@P39: communication networks, and civilian official concerns\nL1701@P39: about the involvement of the police and military in civil\nL1702@P39: power.\nL1703@P39: FCO 141/7135-7136: covers intelligence and other material on internal\nL1704@P39: Somaliland Protectorate and Somalia politics;\nL1705@P39: includes correspondence on Jomo Kenyatta and\nL1706@P39: Ronald Ngalas’ visits to Somalia, Soviet activity in\nL1707@P39: Somalia, and the breaking off of diplomatic relations\nL1708@P39-40: by Somalia in 1963. See also material under Ministry Colonial administration records (migrated archives) guidance\nL1709@P40: of Defence, Intelligence and Security below (FCO\nL1710@P40: 141/7116-7120).\nL1711@P40: Related material: CO 822/2003-2006, 2035-2036, 3030-3038.\nL1712@P40: Kenyan political organisations\nL1713@P40: FCO 141/7111, 7112, 7114: three files (3cm thick in total, 1961-63).\nL1714@P40: FCO 141/7111: covers the prohibition by Kenyatta’s provisional\nL1715@P40: government of a visit by the Conservative MP (and KADU\nL1716@P40: sympathiser and adviser), F.M. Bennett.\nL1717@P40: FCO 141/7112: includes newspaper cuttings, intelligence reports and other\nL1718@P40: material on KADU.\nL1719@P40: FCO 141/7114: contains material on the Kenya Land Freedom Army and\nL1720@P40: other Kikuyu organisations, including a 21 page ‘Report on\nL1721@P40: Kikuyu Subversive Activities’ dated 1961.\nL1722@P40: Related material: CO 822/2114, 2390, 2390, 3275.\nL1723@P40: Kenyan Independence\nL1724@P40: FCO 141/7074-7075, 7082: three files (4.5cm thick, 1962-63).\nL1725@P40: FCO 141/7074: relates to Ugandan and Tanganyikan attitudes towards\nL1726@P40: Kenyan independence, including Obote’s time in Kenya,\nL1727@P40: talks on the East African Federation and the Tanganyikan\nL1728@P40: attitude towards Zanzibar. FCO 141/7075 covers the\nL1729@P40: East African Federation in more detail and the future of\nL1730@P40: the Kenya Protectorate. FCO 141/7082 relates to the\nL1731@P40: negotiation of the Karasuk boundary with Uganda.\nL1732@P40: FCO 141/7076-7080: five files (10cm thick in total, 1962-63) covering\nL1733@P40: discussions and talks on the independence constitution,\nL1734@P40: including papers on divorce law and Commonwealth\nL1735@P40: membership (FCO 141/7076), preliminary and\nL1736@P40: preparatory talks (FCO 141/7077-7079), the\nL1737@P40: Independence Conference (FCO 141/7078, 7079) and\nL1738@P40: a general file including notes of informal discussions\nL1739@P40: alongside the talks and conference, material on the\nL1740@P40: withdrawal of the British military and telegrams to\nL1741@P40: Nairobi giving bulletins of progress in the Independence\nL1742@P40: Conference (FCO 141/7080).\nL1743@P40: FCO 141/7081, 7083, 7084: three files (2cm thick in total, 1962-63) relating\nL1744@P40: to the issue of citizenship in independent Kenya;\nL1745@P40: the agreement for public officers; and pre\u0002independence financial talks respectively.\nL1746@P40: Related material to all the above: CO 822/2033, 2235-2249, 2366-2391, 2736.\nL1747@P40: Formation of KANU Government\nL1748@P40: FCO 141/7109-7110: two files (11cm thick in total, 1963) covering the\nL1749@P40: inauguration of the new government, the processes\nL1750@P40: established for co-operation between the governor and\nL1751@P40: the new government, Special Branch weekly reports\nL1752@P40: to the governor on the new government, ministerial\nL1753@P40: appointments, the East African Federation, administrative\nL1754@P40: changes and the handover of power.\nL1755@P40-41: Related material: CO 822/2390, 3065, 3166, 3167.Colonial administration records (migrated archives) guidance\nL1756@P41: Arab affairs\nL1757@P41: FCO 141/7071-7073, 7113: four files (11cm thick in total, 1961-63).\nL1758@P41: FCO 141/7071: includes the report of the Robertson Commission on the\nL1759@P41: future of the Kenya Coastal Strip (see CO 894 for the\nL1760@P41: papers of the commission; the report was published as\nL1761@P41: Cmnd 1565 of 1961) and related correspondence and\nL1762@P41: papers on the future of the Strip.\nL1763@P41: FCO 141/7072 and 7073: include intelligence reports on Arab views on the\nL1764@P41: Coastal Strip and on the Movement for Coast\nL1765@P41: Autonomy, reports and papers relating to the\nL1766@P41: Coastal Strip conference, and an appeal on the\nL1767@P41: election result in the Bamba constituency, Kilifi\nL1768@P41: County Council (FCO 141/7073 only).\nL1769@P41: FCO 141/7113: relates the visit of the Aga Khan to Kenya and his support\nL1770@P41: for Ismailis in Kenya and South Africa.\nL1771@P41: Related material: CO 822/804, 1295, 2002; CO 894/1-14\nL1772@P41: Land settlement and resettlement\nL1773@P41: FCO 141/6918-6919\nL1774@P41: 2 files (approx 3cm thick each, 1962-63) on the subject of Land settlement\nL1775@P41: schemes: includes discussions and agreements surrounding British financial\nL1776@P41: support to Kenya on independence, in terms of the purchase of European-owned\nL1777@P41: farms and the settlement of Africans. Also contains lists of ‘compassionate\nL1778@P41: cases’; photocopied maps showing numbered farm plots; newspaper clippings;\nL1779@P41: Kikuyu resettlement and issues.\nL1780@P41: FCO 141/6919: also includes reports of problems in various districts and\nL1781@P41: gangs causing ‘security issues’.\nL1782@P41: FCO 141/6920: thin file (less than 40 pages, 1962-63) on abandoned farms.\nL1783@P41: Lists estates abandoned and legal discussions surrounding the\nL1784@P41: scheme to buy and issues of squatters.\nL1785@P41: FCO 141/6921: (3cm thick, 1961-63) files on land and tribal tensions; includes\nL1786@P41: details of political organisations, observations of tribal groups\nL1787@P41: and individuals, reports on illegal squatting and security\nL1788@P41: measures in place, details of tensions in difference provinces\nL1789@P41: and a map of the Rift valley area.\nL1790@P41: Education\nL1791@P41: FCO 141/7051-7053: three files (0.5cm thick in total, 1962-63).\nL1792@P41: FCO 141/7051: includes intelligence reports on Kenyan students\nL1793@P41: studying in Communist countries and in Egypt (covering\nL1794@P41: disturbances in the latter by Kenyan students).\n"
